<exercise xml:id="cubic-spline-coefficients">
<title>Cubic spline: Finding admissible coefficients</title>


  <statement>
    <p>We have the following piecewise cubic polynomial:</p>
    <p>
      <md>
        f(x)=
        \begin{cases}
        2+x+x^2+0.1x^3 &amp; x\leq 1,\\
        b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2-x^3 &amp; x&gt;1.
        \end{cases}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>What are <m>b_0</m>, <m>b_1</m>, and <m>b_2</m> to make this a cubic spline?</p>    
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="recursive-binary-splitting-region">
<title>Recursive binary splitting: 2D example</title>


  <statement>
    <p>Express the shaded region <m>R</m> using set notation <m>R=\{X\mid\ldots\}</m> and sketch the corresponding tree.</p>
    <image source="figures/recursive_binary_splitting_question">
      <shortdescription>Predictor space with a shaded region to be represented using set notation and a decision tree.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="recursive-binary-splitting-3d-region">
<title>Recursive binary splitting: 3D example</title>


  <statement>
    <p>Sketch and shade the region <m>R</m> indicated by the equivalent representations in (a) and (b). </p>
    <image source="figures/recursive_binary_splitting_question3D">
      <shortdescription>Three-dimensional predictor-space representations corresponding to a region to be sketched and shaded.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>R</m> is the intersection of the shaded volumes that correspond to the rectangular prism obtained by filling in the volume described by each level of the tree.
    </p>
    <image source="figures/recursive_binary_splitting_question3D_answer">
      <shortdescription>Three-dimensional predictor-space representations that show the shaded region corresponding to the given tree, along with the three regions (corresponding to the tree levels) whose intersection give the required region.</shortdescription>
      
    </image>
  </answer>
</exercise>

