<exercise>
  <title>SVM: One-Vs-One Classification</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Suppose we want to classify observations into one of four categories: <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>D</m>. Each observation is described by two quantitative predictors, <m>X_1</m> and <m>X_2</m>.</p>
    <p>We use the one-vs-one approach to construct an SVM classifier. </p>
    <p>Suppose the six fitted pairwise SVMs for this problem have the following decision functions:</p>
    <p><md>
      \begin{aligned}
      f_{AB}(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1+X_2-2,\\
      f_{AC}(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1-X_2+0.5,\\
      f_{AD}(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1+X_2-4,\\
      f_{BC}(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1-X_2+0.5,\\
      f_{BD}(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1+X_2-4,\\
      f_{CD}(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1+X_2-2.
      \end{aligned}
    </md></p>
    <p>For each pairwise classifier, a positive decision-function value predicts the first class in the subscript, while a negative value predicts the second class. For example, if <m>f_{AB}(\mathbf{X})&gt;0</m>, the classifier predicts class <m>A</m>; if <m>f_{AB}(\mathbf{X})&lt;0</m>, it predicts class <m>B</m>.</p>
    <p>Consider the new observation</p>
    <p><md>\mathbf{X}^\ast=(X_1^\ast,X_2^\ast)=(1,2).</md></p>
      <p>
        (This was created with the help of ChatGPT).
      </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>How many binary SVM classifiers must be fitted for this problem? List all the pairs of classes that are compared.</p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For one-vs-one classification with SVM we fit a separate binary SVM for every pair of classes. For a problem with <m>K</m> classes, the total number of pairwise classifiers is</p>
    <p><md>\binom{K}{2}=\frac{K(K-1)}{2}.</md></p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>There are <m>K=4</m> classes, so the number of pairwise classifiers is</p>
      <p><md>
        \binom{4}{2}=\frac{4(4-1)}{2}=6.
      </md></p>
      <p>The six pairs of classes are:</p>
      <p><ul>
        <li><p><m>A</m> vs. <m>B</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>A</m> vs. <m>C</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>A</m> vs. <m>D</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>B</m> vs. <m>C</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>B</m> vs. <m>D</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>C</m> vs. <m>D</m></p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Evaluate each of the six decision functions at <m>\mathbf{X}^\ast</m>. For each pairwise classifier, identify which class is predicted.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Substitute <m>X_1^\ast=1</m> and <m>X_2^\ast=2</m> into each decision function:</p>
      <p><md>
        \begin{aligned}
        f_{AB}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1+2-2=1,\\
        f_{AC}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1-2+0.5=-0.5,\\
        f_{AD}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1+2-4=-1,\\
        f_{BC}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1-2+0.5=-0.5,\\
        f_{BD}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1+2-4=-1,\\
        f_{CD}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1+2-2=1.
        \end{aligned}
      </md></p>
      <p>Using the sign of each decision function, the predictions are:</p>
      <p><ul>
        <li><p><m>A</m> vs. <m>B</m>: <m>f_{AB}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&gt;0</m>, so predict <m>A</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>A</m> vs. <m>C</m>: <m>f_{AC}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&lt;0</m>, so predict <m>C</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>A</m> vs. <m>D</m>: <m>f_{AD}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&lt;0</m>, so predict <m>D</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>B</m> vs. <m>C</m>: <m>f_{BC}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&lt;0</m>, so predict <m>C</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>B</m> vs. <m>D</m>: <m>f_{BD}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&lt;0</m>, so predict <m>D</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>C</m> vs. <m>D</m>: <m>f_{CD}(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&gt;0</m>, so predict <m>C</m>.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Construct a vote tally showing the number of votes received by each of the four classes. Which class receives the most votes?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Each pairwise classifier gives one vote to its predicted class. Counting the predictions from the previous part gives:</p>
      <table>
        <title>Vote tally for the test observation</title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Class</cell>
            <cell>Number of votes</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell><m>A</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>B</m></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>C</m></cell><cell>3</cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>D</m></cell><cell>2</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>Class <m>C</m> receives the most votes, with three votes.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Using the one-vs-one classification rule, which class would you assign to the new observation <m>\mathbf{X}^\ast</m>? Explain how the pairwise predictions lead to your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Under the one-vs-one approach, we assign the observation to the class that receives the most votes from the pairwise classifiers. Since class <m>C</m> receives three votes, more than any other class, we classify the new observation as</p>
      <p><md>\boxed{\text{Class }C.}</md></p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <title>SVM: One-Vs-All Classification</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Suppose we want to classify observations into one of four categories: <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>D</m>. Each observation is described by two quantitative predictors, <m>X_1</m> and <m>X_2</m>.</p>
    <p>We use the one-vs-all approach to construct an SVM classifier. </p>
    <p>Suppose the four fitted SVMs for this problem have the following decision functions:</p>
    <p><md>
      \begin{aligned}
      f_A(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= X_1+X_2-4,\\
      f_B(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= 2X_1-X_2+1,\\
      f_C(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= -X_1+2X_2-1,\\
      f_D(\mathbf{X}) &amp;= -X_1-X_2+1.
      \end{aligned}
    </md></p>
    <p>A positive decision-function value indicates that the corresponding classifier predicts its class rather than the remaining classes. Since the classifiers are fitted independently, it is possible for more than one classifier to produce a positive value, or for all classifiers to produce negative values.</p>
    <p>To obtain a final prediction, we assign the observation to the class with the largest decision-function value.</p>
    <!-- <p><md>
      \widehat{Y}=\underset{k\in\{A,B,C,D\}}{\operatorname{argmax}}\ f_k(\mathbf{X}).
    </md></p> -->
    <p>Consider the new observation</p>
    <p><md>
      \mathbf{X}^\ast=(X_1^\ast,X_2^\ast)=(1,2).
    </md></p>
          <p>
        (This was created with the help of ChatGPT).
      </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>How many binary SVM classifiers must be fitted for this problem? Describe how the classes are grouped for each classifier.</p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        In this approach, we fit one binary SVM for each class, treating that class as the positive class and all remaining classes as the negative class. Thus, for a problem with <m>K</m> classes, we fit <m>K</m> binary SVM classifiers.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>There are <m>K=4</m> classes, so we fit four binary SVM classifiers. Each classifier separates one class from all the remaining classes:</p>
      <p><ul>
        <li><p>Classifier A: class <m>A</m> vs. classes <m>B,C,D</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Classifier B: class <m>B</m> vs. classes <m>A,C,D</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Classifier C: class <m>C</m> vs. classes <m>A,B,D</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Classifier D: class <m>D</m> vs. classes <m>A,B,C</m>.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Evaluate each of the four decision functions at <m>\mathbf{X}^\ast</m>. For each classifier, determine whether its decision-function value is positive or negative. Which classifiers predict their corresponding class?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Substitute <m>X_1^\ast=1</m> and <m>X_2^\ast=2</m> into each decision function:</p>
      <p><md>
        \begin{aligned}
        f_A(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1+2-4=-1,\\
        f_B(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=2(1)-2+1=1,\\
        f_C(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=-1+2(2)-1=2,\\
        f_D(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=-1-2+1=-2.
        \end{aligned}
      </md></p>
      <p>The results are summarized below:</p>
      <table>
        <title>One-vs-all decision-function values</title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Classifier</cell>
            <cell>Decision value</cell>
            <cell>Prediction</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>A</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell>Not A</cell></row>
          <row><cell>B</cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell>B</cell></row>
          <row><cell>C</cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell>C</cell></row>
          <row><cell>D</cell><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell>Not D</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>Classifiers B and C produce positive decision-function values and therefore predict their corresponding classes.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Using the one-vs-all classification rule, which class would you assign to the new observation <m>\mathbf{X}^\ast</m>? Explain why.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>We assign the observation to the class with the largest decision-function value. The four values are</p>
      <p><md>
        \begin{aligned}
        f_A(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=-1,\\
        f_B(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=1,\\
        f_C(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=2,\\
        f_D(\mathbf{X}^\ast)&amp;=-2.
        \end{aligned}
      </md></p>
      <p>Since <m>f_C(\mathbf{X}^\ast)=2</m> is the largest value, we classify the new observation as</p>
      <p><md>\boxed{\text{Class }C.}</md></p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>



