<exercise>
  <title>NN: Multiple outcomes</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Given the shown neural net, answer the following questions. Here <m>M</m> is the number of possible outcomes, <m>K</m> is the number of hidden units, and <m>p</m> is the number of predictors.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Neural network for classification.</caption>
      <image source="figures/NN_classification_WS">
        <shortdescription>Neural network architecture for a classification problem.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>M</m>, <m>K</m>, and <m>p</m> for this example. How many hidden layers are there?</p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Write the equation for the ReLU activation function <m>g(z)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Compute <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> using the following weights and equations. Use ReLU as the activation function <m>g(z)</m>, and set <m>\mathbf{X}=(1,1)</m>. Can you do this using the matrix-vector product we learned too?</p>
      <p><md>
        w=\begin{bmatrix}
        -1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1\\
        2 &amp; 3 &amp; -4
        \end{bmatrix}
      </md></p>
      <p><md>
        A_k=h_k(\mathbf{X})=g\left(w_{k0}+\sum_{j=1}^{p}w_{kj}X_j\right)
      </md></p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>This neural net is actually trying to <em>classify</em> the output as either "type 1" (<m>Y_0</m>) or "type 2" (<m>Y_1</m>). We need an <m>M\times(K+1)</m> matrix <m>\boldsymbol{\beta}</m>, which is <m>2\times3</m> for this neural net. Compute the temporary variable <m>Z_m</m> for each possible outcome. We will next use this variable to compute the probability that the observation belongs to each of the two classes.</p>
      <p><md>
        Z_m=\beta_{m0}+\sum_{\ell=1}^{K}\beta_{m\ell}A_\ell,
        \qquad
        \boldsymbol{\beta}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        2 &amp; -1 &amp; -3\\
        2 &amp; 3 &amp; 4
        \end{bmatrix}
      </md></p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>We now have <m>M</m> real numbers <m>Z_1,\ldots,Z_M</m> corresponding to <m>M</m> possible categorical outcomes. We need a way to convert these <m>M</m> numbers to a probability distribution over <m>M</m> possible outcomes. This can be accomplished using the softmax function <m>f_m(\mathbf{X})</m> shown below. Compute <m>f_m</m> for each of the two possible outcomes.</p>
      <p><md>
        f_m(\mathbf{X})=\operatorname{Pr}(Y=m\mid\mathbf{X})
        =\frac{e^{Z_m}}{\sum_{\ell=0}^{M}e^{Z_\ell}}
      </md></p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>The last step is to predict the class for the given observation. Which class would you predict and why?</p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <title>NN: Identifying problem type</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Given the shown neural net, answer the following questions.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Neural network with multiple hidden layers for regression.</caption>
      <image source="figures/NN_multiLayer_regression_WS">
        <shortdescription>Neural network architecture with multiple hidden layers.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>How many hidden layers are there?</p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>M</m> and <m>p</m> for this example. Do you think this is a classification or regression problem? Why?</p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Compute all the <m>A</m>'s. The subscript of <m>K</m> is the corresponding number of hidden units of that layer; for example, <m>K_1</m> is the number of hidden units in layer 1 (<m>L_1</m>), etc.</p>
      <p><md>
        A^{(1)}_k=h^{(1)}_k(\mathbf{X})
        =g\left(w^{(1)}_{k0}+\sum_{j=1}^{p}w^{(1)}_{kj}X_j\right)
      </md></p>
      <p><md>
        A^{(2)}_\ell=h^{(2)}_\ell(\mathbf{X})
        =g\left(w^{(2)}_{\ell 0}+\sum_{k=1}^{K_1}w^{(2)}_{\ell k}A^{(1)}_k\right)
      </md></p>
      <p>Use the weights</p>
      <p><md>
        L_1:\quad w^{(1)}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\\
        0 &amp; -5 &amp; 1\\
        2 &amp; 3 &amp; -1
        \end{bmatrix}
      </md></p>
      <p><md>
        L_2:\quad w^{(2)}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp; 0 &amp; -3 &amp; 1\\
        -1 &amp; 4 &amp; 0 &amp; -2
        \end{bmatrix}
      </md></p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Predict <m>Y</m> for <m>\mathbf{X}=(1,1)</m>. Use the <m>\boldsymbol{\beta}</m> vector below.</p>
      <p><md>
        \boldsymbol{\beta}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 &amp; -1 &amp; -2
        \end{bmatrix}
      </md></p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>

